<!-- Teaching layer: android/testing/ui-testing.md; state/callback contract first, platform evidence separately. -->
<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The checkout button looks perfect in a screenshot, yet tapping it never submits the order. The pixels were correct; the connection from a user action to a callback was broken. A useful UI test first names which of those promises it must prove.</p>

Start with controlled screen state and a captured callback. Move outward to navigation, dependency injection and a device only when the required behavior crosses those boundaries. Visual review remains a separate form of evidence: correct text and click semantics do not prove readable layout.

<!-- @diagram 169407b7471ca5c4 -->
### Choose the cheapest layer that can see the failure

<figure class="viz"><div class="node is-key" style="max-width:22rem;margin:0 auto .6rem"><strong>What confidence is needed?</strong></div><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:4"><div class="node"><strong>State and semantics</strong><small>Compose UI test</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Android integration on the JVM</strong><small>Robolectric</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Visual regression</strong><small>Paparazzi / screenshot test</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Framework + device behaviour</strong><small>instrumented device test, then a small representative device matrix</small></div></div><figcaption>Redrawn from the original decision diagram.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after The UI-Test Landscape :: visual -->
### Three different witnesses

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Semantics + actions</strong><small>text, enabled state, click result</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Rendered image</strong><small>clipping, spacing, theme, typography</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Platform integration</strong><small>system UI, permissions, real services</small></div></div><figcaption>Choose the cheapest sufficient witness; timings in the original tool table are examples, not benchmarks for your project.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Real Patterns :: animation -->
### Trace one click through a state-driven screen

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Trace one click through a state-driven screen</span><div class="stage viz-flow"><div class="node" data-el="state" data-art="flow" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Controlled state</strong><small data-text="stateV">can submit</small></div><div class="node" data-el="ui" data-art="screen" data-art-role="client"><strong>Submit button</strong><small data-text="uiV">enabled</small></div><div class="node" data-el="callback" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Captured callback</strong><small data-text="callbackV">not called</small></div><div class="node" data-el="assert" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Assertion</strong><small data-text="assertV">pending</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Render known state", "caption": "Render the plain screen with a known enabled state and a callback that records the order ID. No application graph is needed for this wiring contract.", "state": {"state": "active", "ui": "active"}}, {"label": "Find and act", "caption": "Find the button through the semantics tree and call performClick(), which on Android injects a touch click at that node. The click is the input; a screenshot alone cannot exercise it.", "state": {"ui": "done", "callback": "active"}, "text": {"callbackV": "order-42 recorded"}}, {"label": "Assert the public effect", "caption": "Check that the callback received order-42. If the screen also changes state, observe that state with the appropriate Compose synchronization.", "state": {"assert": "done"}, "text": {"assertV": "expected callback value"}}, {"label": "Choose the next boundary", "caption": "This proves screen wiring, not payment success. A server contract or device journey must earn its place with a different assertion.", "state": {"state": "done", "ui": "done", "callback": "done"}, "text": {"assertV": "scope of evidence recorded"}}]</script><figcaption>A callback test and a screenshot test can share controlled input while proving different promises.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Real Patterns :: check -->
### Check which witness you need

<details><summary>A screenshot matches. Has the click callback been proved?</summary>

No. Exercise the action and observe the callback or resulting public behavior.
</details>

<details><summary>A semantic assertion passes. Can text still be clipped?</summary>

Yes. Add rendered-image evidence for visual contracts and review the diff.
</details>

<details><summary>When should a state-driven component test become an integration test?</summary>

When the contract depends on navigation, lifecycle, DI or a platform boundary rather than the plain UI input/output seam.
</details>


